      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s name honors John Armstrong, the Secretary of War under President James Madison.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fort_Armstrong_(Illinois)",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Fort Armstrong, built 1816–1836, served as a frontier defense and headquarters during the Black Hawk War.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18,
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fort_Armstrong_(Illinois)",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The fort sat at the foot of Rock Island, overlooking the Mississippi River near today’s Quad Cities.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fort_Armstrong_(Illinois)",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Fort Armstrong measured 300 feet square, constructed of stone and timber with three large blockhouses at its corners.",
